// #2760 RC leak guard: a wrapper that binds the borrow's OWN SOURCE.
//
// The retain #2760 adds for a wrapped borrow must not fire when the spine
// itself binds what the borrow comes from. `x = { let ys = [..];
// Array::get(ys, 0) }` is already promoted by the inner `ELet` lowering, which
// retains the escaping element before dropping `ys` (keyed on
// `scope_tail_proj_root(body) == n`). Retaining it a second time gives the slot
// two references while its drop spends one, so every assignment leaks the
// element permanently.
//
// Found in review on #2783, not by a gate -- the answer stays CORRECT, so only
// a heap bound can see it. Measured on `6507c60` at N=20000:
//
//   | compiler                              | heap_used   |
//   |---------------------------------------|------------:|
//   | unpatched                             |       164 B |
//   | #2760 classifying from the leaf callee | 1,600,084 B |
//   | with the source test (this tree)       |       164 B |
//
// 80 B per iteration, so it SCALES -- the middle row is 20000 x 80 plus the
// steady state, and the bound below separates it by four orders of magnitude.
//
// The discriminating fact is whether the binder the borrow's root names
// ACQUIRED AN OWNED REFERENCE -- not merely whether the spine bound that name,
// which is what the first version of this guard tested. `ys` here is bound to
// an array literal, so `classify_let_value_heap` gives it `rc_kind = 2` and the
// inner lowering promotes; `xs` in the issue's own repro is a parameter, so
// nothing promoted and the retain is still required.
//
// A THIRD case sits between them and is why the name alone will not do: a
// binder that is itself a borrow (`let ys = Array::get(xss, 0)`) stays at
// `rc_kind == 0`, gets no scope-end drop, and promotes nothing -- so declining
// there frees a reference the container still owns. Measured at 850 against the
// bump lane's 770; it is asserted by value in
// `rc_wrapper_assign_borrow_test.vibe` (`assign_borrow_bound_binder`), because
// that error shows up as a wrong answer and not as a leak.
//
// The two directions are opposite errors and each needs its own lane: this file
// bounds the leak, `rc_wrapper_assign_borrow_test.vibe` asserts the values. Both
// files carry BOTH leaf kinds since #2796 -- the call and the projection are
// answered by different arms of `spine_leaf_unowned`, so a lane that holds only
// one of them is a lane the other arm can walk past.
//
// `main` returns 0: every shape here adds 0, so the answer cannot drift with
// the allocation behaviour being measured.
enum Node {
  Leaf(Int);
  Pair(Node, Node)
}

struct Holder {
  node: Node
}

fn once() -> Int {
  let mut x = Pair(Leaf(1), Leaf(2))
  x = {
    let ys = [Pair(Leaf(3), Leaf(4))]
    Array::get(ys, 0)
  }
  0
}

// The same shape with a PROJECTION leaf instead of a borrow-returning call.
// `spine_leaf_unowned` answers these in two separate arms, so a suppression
// correct in one is not thereby correct in the other -- #2796 was exactly that,
// the arms disagreeing, and this row is the leak side of the arm it fixed.
// Without a shape here, the projection arm had a value lane
// (`rc_wrapper_assign_borrow_test.vibe`) and no leak lane, which is the
// asymmetry that let #2760 ship its leak in the first place.
fn once_proj() -> Int {
  let mut x = Pair(Leaf(1), Leaf(2))
  x = {
    let h = Holder::{ node: Pair(Leaf(3), Leaf(4)) }
    h.node
  }
  0
}

export fn main() -> Int {
  let mut i = 0
  while i < 20000 {
    let r = once()
    let rp = once_proj()
    i = i + r + rp + 1
  }
  0
}
